# A BOUND METHOD stored in a field, taken off a freshly constructed receiver:
# `self.cb = Tagged(tag).m1`. The field was not registered at all unless the
# constructor's ARGUMENT was a LITERAL -- a module global, a parameter and
# `self.tag` all failed identically, the call fell to run-time dispatch on the
# receiver, and it died with `TypeError: object is not callable`.
#
# PyCtorSelectorType chases `Foo()` through its trailing selectors. A selector
# followed by `(` is looked up as a METHOD; a selector WITHOUT one was looked up
# only as a FIELD, so a bound-method REFERENCE resolved to nothing and the chase
# answered tyUnknown. A bound method is a CALLABLE VALUE and a callable value
# travels as a variant -- the rule PyInferExprType's own lambda arm already
# states, here for the third construct that needs it.
#
# EVERY ROW IS AT ONE ARGUMENT, DELIBERATELY. The defect is arity-independent,
# and it was MASKED by the four-argument cap on callable fields
# (bug-n-a-callable-attribute-dispatched-at-run-time-takes-at-most-4-arguments,
# fixed the same day): with that cap in place this same call reported `takes at
# most 4 arguments` instead, so a wide-arity fixture measures the two together
# and cannot say which one it is pinning. One argument reaches only this.
#
# The receiver must arrive through a CONTAINER. `o = mod.Holder("B")` is typed
# statically and dispatches without consulting the field table at all, which is
# the shape in which every spelling below already printed the right answer.

import boundmethodfield_mod

hs = boundmethodfield_mod.holders("P")

# The two that worked, FIRST -- so a stale scan still answers here and nothing
# below is carried by a warm path.
print(hs[0].lit(1))
print(hs[0].num(2))

# The three that did not.
print(hs[0].glb(3))
print(hs[0].par(4))
print(hs[0].att(5))

# The control: same receiver expression, bound off a local instead.
print(hs[0].loc(6))

# A second receiver, to show the field is registered on the CLASS and not
# rescued per instance.
js = boundmethodfield_mod.holders("Q")
print(js[0].par(7))
print(js[0].att(8))
